{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-14_14-15-46-hashicorp_raft-real-run/draft; sed -n '105,115p' update.json\"","aggregated_output":"          \"entry_id\": \"b14455c083ad486396c1ab58a8462762-knowledge-1\",\n          \"revision\": 1,\n          \"assertion\": \"Twelve independent trials each confirmed between eight and ten times with the nonvoter as the only reachable peer.\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"conclusion\": \"holds\",\n      \"defect_claim\": \"asserted\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Revision of this question's account with the repeated measurement. The mechanism stands and is now supported by breadth rather than by a single trial: in twelve independent interrupted trials the cut-off leader was confirmed on every poll it took except two, each trial producing eight to ten confirmations spread over roughly 45ms with the tally at 2 against a quorum of 2 and only the nonvoter on the leader's side of the partition. Because the routes to the other voters stayed removed for the whole window, those sustained confirmations cannot be explained by pre-partition traffic, which is exactly the explanation that the violated control in the same run forces me to take seriously for first-poll observations. The defect claim is therefore asserted for the confirmation primitive: an acknowledgement from a server that cannot vote satisfies the voter quorum, so a successful VerifyLeader does not establish that the leader can still reach a voter majority. The consequence half of the earlier account is corrected rather than repeated: the child question's repeated measurement shows the confirmation window closing before a competing leader exists in this configuration family, so the earlier reading that a confirmed cut-off leader coexists with a newer commit is bounded out for these timer relations, and the surviving consequence is the unsound confirmation itself, bounded by the leader's own lease. The earlier single-trial control result (zero confirmations) is retained as an observation from its own receipt but is not used as support here, because the repeated run shows that a first poll in the control can be satisfied before the partition takes effect.\",\n      \"counterevidence\": [\n        \"The reading depends on the nonvoter's acknowledgement being counted, which the interrupted trials establish by construction and by the tally; the record does not show which peer answered, so the attribution rests on the partition plus the tally rather than on a per-ack label.\",\n        \"A deployment without nonvoters cannot reach the path, since the extra acknowledgement must come from a server whose Suffrage is not Voter.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
